# Working with Montra

Montra turns narrated screen recordings into implementation-ready documents made of
tickets. A ticket body is a markdown implementation spec, written from what the person
said and did on screen. The Montra tools read and update documents, tickets, ticket
images, and transcripts in the one workspace the connection was authorized for.

## Treat the connection as read-only

The tickets are the person's spec. Report progress in conversation, not by editing
Montra.

- Do not call `create_document`, `update_document`, `create_ticket`, `update_ticket`, or
  `delete_ticket` unless the user explicitly asks for that change in the current
  conversation.
- Never mark a ticket implemented, done, or complete by rewriting its title, summary, or
  body. There is no ticket status to set, and an unrequested edit overwrites what the
  person wrote.
- `link_ticket_issue` is the exception: when the user asks you to create a Linear or
  Notion issue from a ticket, recording that link is part of the export — do it without a
  separate ask.

## Getting context

- Start from `list_documents` or `get_document`.
- `get_ticket_images` returns the annotated screenshots behind a ticket. They usually
  carry the detail the text leaves implicit — look at them before deciding scope.
- `get_transcript` returns the narration, with timestamps, when the wording of a ticket
  is ambiguous.
- When the user pastes a Montra share link (`montra.com/share/...` or
  `/tasks-for-agents/...`), call `get_document_by_share_url` with it instead of fetching
  the web page. It resolves links in any workspace, and invite-only links resolve as the
  signed-in user.
